The original SDK owns its one-output swapchain scheduling, generated/real presentation order, pacing, UI composition, debug pacing overlay and memory accounting. The SDK is source-built for x64 and WoW64; this is not loading an external vendor renderer. Local adaptations remove legacy foreign-private-C++ ABI calls, old SDK interface versions and AntiLag vendor transport, use owned COM identity and allocation queries, and accommodate llvm-mingw headers. Original ResizeBuffers is retained. The original ResizeBuffers1 success-only stub is replaced by E_NOTIMPL. Original asynchronous callback/Present errors are not exposed as a new wait-for-presents error protocol.

Shared public FSR and NGX generation translates through the existing WMTMetal4FG bridge to native MetalFX. MetalFX owns interpolation; no AMD optical-flow/interpolation shaders, custom multi-phase extension, output bank, GPU actual-count protocol or scene-cut classifier is retained. Resource/format/color-space/depth/motion-vector/rectangle/UI mapping, input snapshots, native history resets, queue ordering and GPU-retirement ownership remain translation responsibilities. Public FSR debug flags map through the existing native bridge's diagnostic overlays, not the removed AMD generation algorithm.

Only one output is supported. FSR requests for two through four outputs return NO_PROVIDER; NGX advertises MultiFrameCountMax=1 and rejects higher count/index values. The native API writes an output texture but exposes no generated-versus-copied result or actual-count buffer. Bootstrap/reset output can equal current color; an SDK generated-role callback does not prove a new image or physical display. A non-null NGX OutputDisableInterpolation request is rejected rather than fabricated. No independent scene-cut or multi-frame algorithm fills these native API gaps.

FSR requires Configure before direct Prepare and a matching current-epoch Prepare for Generate. A newer Prepare does not replace the captured inputs of an earlier matching frame. Disabling clears prepared metadata and invalidates history. Already recorded commands retain their resources/context through execution and retirement; closed-list replay encodes native work again, not an immutable-result/no-op contract. At most 64 distinct unretired configurations are retained. Rectangle borders preserve current color; native generated pixels, history/reset, debug overlays and lifetime are tested separately from SDK callback ordering and native drawable display.

Public DLL ordinals preserve the reference Wine spec: Configure=1, CreateContext=2, DestroyContext=3, Dispatch=4, Query=5. Only the owned D3D12 renderer is accepted. There is no GPTK/D3DMetal dependency, shader converter or foreign-backend fallback. No ordinary DXGI swapchain injection is installed; NGX output presentation and pacing belong to its caller.
